WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.900
- Hey guys, you've been asking
for me to make more content.

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.770
So let's do it!

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.270
Every Wednesday 8 p.m.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.200
we're gonna have a new episode for you,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.430
where I'm gonna tackle
the newest headlines,

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.080
the craziest articles,

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.700
maybe touch on some research,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.780
and give you some updates
on what I'm up to.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.640
To kick off this Wednesday's show,

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.790
I decided to watch a medical drama

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:20.350
that just came out last night.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.467
You've been asking for it,

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:22.760
and you got it.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.396
New Amsterdam, let's go.

00:00:24.396 --> 00:00:25.229
Bee-woop!

00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:26.830
- Dr. Bloom, she's dead.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.250
No pulse, no BP, cyanotic, rigor mortis.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.770
- I need a defib ventilator, ALS,

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.020
and resuscitation team. Now.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.606
- That's impossible, she's dead.

00:00:34.606 --> 00:00:36.330
- Well, at least you
didn't kill her twice.

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.490
- If she wasn't dead,

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:40.310
you need to start doing chest compressions

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:41.730
if you're getting a defibrillator.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.100
So, I'm not sure what
happened in that scene.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.000
- [Max] Going somewhere?

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.000
- [Helen] I have to tape segments

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.610
with The Morning Show and Megyn Kelly,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:49.920
then give the keynote address

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.559
at the Oncology Conference in Vail.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:52.392
- [Max] I'll take that for you.

00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:53.750
- [Helen] Thank you.
- I've done a couple segments

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.985
on Megyn Kelly.

00:00:54.985 --> 00:00:57.550
- [Helen] (laughs) You're
funny. I'll be back next week.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.570
We'll lunch at the tavern. My treat.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:01.360
- [Max] Unlike the
previous medical director,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.840
I actually expect you to practice medicine

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.070
at this hospital because, I don't know,

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:07.923
that's your job?

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.780
- You're funny.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:12.613
- You said that.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.460
- I'll tell you what, I'll
continue giving speeches

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.680
all over the world
because you can't afford

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.020
the kind of publicity
I give this hospital.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.320
- I understand what
they're trying to set up.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.570
One doctor actually wants
to practice medicine,

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.420
the other doctor's trying
to be more media savvy.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.390
This is something I have
to reevaluate sometimes,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.040
like how much time am I spending

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.010
practicing medicine versus
doing media and social media?

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.820
I think it is important to do media,

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:38.650
as long as you're doing it the right way.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.040
Because you can deliver a
very meaningful health message

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.150
to millions of people instead of the 30-40

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.910
that you may see in your office.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.160
Plus you can get notoriety and funding

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.083
for your hospital, for a non-profit,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.460
for disease research.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.460
I think those are very
useful uses of social media

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:03.910
and media in general that
sometimes go overlooked.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.090
While I don't necessarily agree

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.490
with how she presents herself,

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:09.810
I kind of understand her message

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:10.690
and where she's coming from.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.750
- Will everyone in the
Cardiac Surgical Department

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.730
please raise your hands?

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.460
Don't be shy, just get 'em up there.

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.293
Great.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.600
Great, thank you. You're all fired.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.860
(crowd murmurs)

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.890
Any department who places
billing above care,

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.450
no matter how much money
you make this hospital,

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:30.600
you will be terminated.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.980
Oh I am serious.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.950
- Whoa, I doubt that that's what happens.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.480
Just because, number one,
people have contracts.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.850
And number two, you're gonna
be hurting a lot of people

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.130
who need cardiac care that
won't be able to get it

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:45.040
from your hospital because
you just fired everybody, so--

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.610
- [Iggy] Can we do something
about healthy food?

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.462
- What do you wanna do about it?

00:02:49.462 --> 00:02:50.590
- Uh, have some.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:51.423
(laughter)

00:02:51.423 --> 00:02:52.320
- That's so true.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.770
If you walk into any hospital cafeteria,

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.410
it's all wings, burgers, fries,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.780
and it makes no sense

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.650
because they just walked
out of their meetings

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.660
with their doctors and maybe
are even out of surgery

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.520
and we're telling them, "You
need to eat healthy food."

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.670
And then when they walk
into the hospital cafeteria,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.460
they're bombarded with fried stuff.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:12.540
It makes no sense.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.590
- [Max] So just tell me what you need,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.490
what your patients need.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:15.680
and I don't care if it's not covered,

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.960
I don't care if the board said no.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.220
Let's get into some trouble.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.690
Let's be

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.660
doctors again.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.270
- It's incredibly dramatized.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.290
Unfortunately a medical director

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.040
can make all those decisions,

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:29.910
because as much as we
want to be self-righteous

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.650
and help everyone and change the system,

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.150
it takes work, it's a
step-by-step process,

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:35.667
it's not, "Let's have one meeting,

00:03:35.667 --> 00:03:37.700
"and I'll change everything
because I'm Superman."

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.200
- I read your files, you
have the lowest billing rates

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.033
in the department.

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:42.120
What's wrong, you don't like surgery?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.100
- I love surgery.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:43.933
- [Max] Then why did you perform

00:03:43.933 --> 00:03:46.170
half as many procedures
as your colleagues?

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.340
- Because my patients didn't need them.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.650
Because there's other ways to help people

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.820
than by cutting them open.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.520
- It's a very important statement to make.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.140
One of the things I try
encourage my patients to do

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.857
when speaking with their
surgeons is to ask a question,

00:03:59.857 --> 00:04:03.260
"Do I actually need this
procedure or surgery?"

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.897
Because a lot of times,
as the saying goes,

00:04:05.897 --> 00:04:07.780
"If you're a hammer, everything's a nail."

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.460
When you're a surgeon,
you're meant to operate,

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.270
and this isn't to down-talk surgeons,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:14.650
because what they do is amazing
and incredibly necessary.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.160
But there are times where there
are unnecessary procedures

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:18.993
being performed.

00:04:18.993 --> 00:04:21.390
The first procedure that comes to mind

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.280
that's been statistically
probably over-performed

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.640
in our society is cardiac catheterization,

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.110
where they put a stent in your heart.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.790
And I'm not talking
about putting a stent in

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:32.500
when someone's actively
having a heart attack,

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.920
that's obviously crucial.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.190
But I'm talking about in patients

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.320
where there's not a clear benefit,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.040
or at least not a clear benefit

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.240
over doing just medical therapy,
giving them medications,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.430
changing their lifestyle,
that sort of thing.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.060
And cardiac catheterizations
are a huge moneymaker

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.130
for hospitals.

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:51.310
So you see hospitals shift their focus

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:52.920
on performing these procedures

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.020
and not focus on prevention,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.120
on focusing on lifestyle factors.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.160
- Stop.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.210
Everyone, back away from the patient.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.842
Masks on. Now.

00:05:02.842 --> 00:05:05.370
(faint speaking)

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.770
- Does he have tuberculosis?

00:05:08.195 --> 00:05:09.390
(phone ringing)
- [Nurse] Check.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:10.920
- Ebola?
- [Nurse] Nothing left here.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:11.980
- What's going on here?
- This is Max.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.320
- We have a patient in
the ED from Liberia,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.910
presenting with all the signs of malaria,

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.192
Lassa Fever, TB, or--

00:05:19.192 --> 00:05:20.025
- Ebola.

00:05:20.025 --> 00:05:21.190
- Oh no!

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.530
- [Max] The first thing we
do is call the mayor's office

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.480
and the CDC if we even
suspect Ebola virus.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.870
Now, the patient is in isolation chamber

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.490
with negative pressure air flow.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.980
That chamber is equipped
with every available medicine

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.257
should the patient need himself medicated.

00:05:35.257 --> 00:05:36.650
- [Team Member] What about us?

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:39.040
- Ebola isn't airborne, but
if you develop symptoms,

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:39.990
you will join him.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:42.340
Until then you will help him.

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.500
Everyone interacting with the patient

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.090
is required to wear personal
protective equipment

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.610
at all times, no shortcuts, ever.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:51.210
- So this is interesting
because Ebola is a disease

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.330
caused by a virus.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.260
It causes a high fever.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.950
It actually causes you
to bleed internally,

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.000
which leads to organ failure.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.740
It doesn't really present
with any unique symptoms:

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.020
abdominal pain, headaches,
muscular pain, fevers,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.350
low blood pressure.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.510
So it's difficult to understand

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.320
if someone has Ebola or something else.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.330
You have to rule it out.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.240
The reason why they suspect
it in this gentleman

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.230
is because when they
opened his documentation,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.810
they saw where he came from.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.450
And in an area where you
have high rates of Ebola,

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.780
like in West Africa,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.580
you automatically wanna
assume that someone has Ebola

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.040
so that it doesn't
contaminate everybody else.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.540
We don't have evidence of
it spreading through air,

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.520
but we have evidence, a lot of evidence,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.500
of it spreading through
blood, mucus, semen,

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.400
basically bodily fluids.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.390
And why Ebola's dangerous?

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:43.760
It has a death rate of
basically one in two.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.720
And even higher in some circumstances.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.120
- You know pens and pencils aren't allowed

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.210
outside the classroom.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.000
- The pen's attached to my journal.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.240
- Okay, you'll have to
give me the journal then.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:53.150
- No.

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.760
- [Teacher] You'll get
it back in the morning.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:56.593
- It's mine.

00:06:56.593 --> 00:06:58.560
- Jemma, give me the journal.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.034
Jemma, don't do this.

00:07:00.034 --> 00:07:01.949
Jemma, please--

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:03.719
- [Jemma] It's mine!

00:07:03.719 --> 00:07:05.310
(Jemma screaming)
(bystanders murmuring)

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:06.260
- So when you have a patient

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.680
who has a psychiatric condition,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.330
it's important to not be provocative

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.940
in any situation because
the situation can escalate

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:15.710
a lot quicker than it would with someone

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.070
who doesn't have that condition.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:20.420
In this case, perhaps
some more conversation,

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.520
maybe calling up the doctor,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.410
because she has such a
great relationship with him,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.270
could've fostered better communication

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.840
and had her give up the pen

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:30.590
without causing that much of a scene,

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:32.930
and her actually hurting
a healthcare provider.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.040
So, it was handled poorly,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:36.830
but obviously healthcare worker abuse

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:38.660
is not welcome in any situation.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.210
- Jemma?

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:41.740
- Keep it.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:43.517
Like, what's the point?

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:52.218
(slams book)

00:07:53.292 --> 00:07:55.773
- Return them to baseline and
put them through the system.

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:57.963
That's my job.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.663
- Well,

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.603
if you can't help Jemma as a doctor,

00:08:03.665 --> 00:08:05.890
then just help her as a human being.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:07.190
- Am I allowed to do that?

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:09.890
- You are now.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:11.760
- Well I always say be a human first.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.050
One of the most overlooked
parts of being a doctor

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.340
is helping someone human to human.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.980
A lot of times, because
we have so many patients

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.550
to take care of, we lose
ourselves in the job

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.490
and focus on checking
boxes, and making sure

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.020
that we write our notes carefully
so that we don't get sued.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:29.860
But in reality we have to not forget

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.050
that we're taking care
of another human being.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:35.103
- [Max] Mrs. Marteen, you could stay here.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.340
But in this case, the treatment
is worse than the disease.

00:08:41.410 --> 00:08:42.623
And with so little time,

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:46.280
maybe there's someplace
else you'd rather be?

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:47.610
- Not every patient wants to spend

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:50.730
their last few months
or years in a hospital

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.220
going in and out of treatments,

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.130
so it's good that he's doing that,

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.170
although this is very,
very, very simplified

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:01.360
because she should have an
evaluation by an oncologist,

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.190
who's a professional in that specialty

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.150
who can decide whether or not the patient

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.730
would benefit or would not
benefit from treatment.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.840
She should maybe even
get a second opinion,

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:13.940
because, as it's shown before,

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:15.040
doctors make mistakes.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.560
Maybe this doctor's mistaken.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.330
So, I agree with the idea
of giving patient options,

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:23.231
but I do not agree with the
way he's going about it.

00:09:23.231 --> 00:09:24.187
(helicopter whirring)

00:09:24.187 --> 00:09:26.870
(machine beeps)

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:29.010
- Oh no.
- Hemoptysis, he's crashing!

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:31.730
- It looks like Ebola
because he's bleeding out.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:35.370
Hemoptysis, meaning that
he's coughing up blood.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.970
I'm not sure if that's
more he's vomiting blood

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:39.540
than coughing up blood.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:41.300
But I wouldn't say he's crashing.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.790
Crashing means his heart's stopping.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:46.973
So, I would call this
more of a rapid response.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.150
So this is very dangerous
because protocol is now gonna say

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:56.213
that she has to be kept in
observation and isolation.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.110
Because who knows if she contracted Ebola,

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.950
and if she did, then she will bring it

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:02.860
to other people in the United States,

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.332
and it could start an epidemic.

00:10:04.332 --> 00:10:06.999
(machine beeps)

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.690
- [Max] Lauren?

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:12.670
- [Lauren] I know what
you're gonna say, that was--

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:14.803
that was stupid, I violated protocol.

00:10:16.042 --> 00:10:17.183
- (clears throat) Lauren,

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:19.603
your glove.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.660
- [Max] Alaim, that shot
they gave you in Liberia,

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.000
they might have thought it was Ebola,

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:29.450
but it wasn't.

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:32.093
It was the Lassa virus,
which is just as deadly.

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:35.810
Except, as Dr. Bloom can tell you,

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.750
the Lassa virus can be treated

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:41.187
with antiviral medication,
which we gave you.

00:10:41.187 --> 00:10:44.790
- You still probably should
have him in isolation,

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.180
just because you don't want
the rest of the staff to get it

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:48.480
and need antiviral medication.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.453
- [Helen] You need to slow down.

00:10:51.457 --> 00:10:53.311
- Yeah.

00:10:53.311 --> 00:10:54.197
I get that a lot.

00:10:54.197 --> 00:10:55.363
- You have cancer.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.463
Squamous cell carcinoma.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.270
- How does she know that?
- But you knew that,

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.103
didn't you?

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:13.963
How can I help?

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:20.250
- I applaud this show for taking a stance

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.160
on showing all of the difficult things

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:23.430
that can happen within medicine,

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.410
how we definitely need
to switch things up,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.110
and it gets me excited that
people are talking about this.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.470
This show obviously has a lot
of dramatic twists and turns.

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:33.260
All in all, I'm pleased with this show.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:34.880
I think it was fun to watch,

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.670
I wanna see more episodes.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:37.900
I wanna know what you guys thought.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:39.640
So jump into the comment section,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:40.830
tell me what you thought,

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.830
tell me if you want me to
watch more of this show,

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.510
because I think I'm a New
Amsterdam fan right now.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:47.710
And I like the acting.

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.230
I think it's well acted and
well written that's for sure.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:54.360
This is the first video
for my little video series

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:55.690
that's gonna happen every Wednesday

00:11:55.690 --> 00:11:57.630
at 8 p.m. Eastern Standard Time.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:00.420
I think I'm gonna call
it Wednesday's Checkup.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:01.730
Let me know if you like that title

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:02.760
or if you want something different.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.750
Again, jump in the comment section.

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:07.298
And as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:12:07.298 --> 00:12:09.798
(light music)

